=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "c631536a9420efbc6a0de73de8e499a41b4407b9198b3b9e57af4d9395ee24d5" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-41263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "c631536a9420efbc6a0de73de8e499a41b4407b9198b3b9e57af4d9395ee24d5" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-41263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8245313d6a1a9363cc6b5889ea8e012c290586419323a4fbba4aa5e1cfd1f2de" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-41263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "c631536a9420efbc6a0de73de8e499a41b4407b9198b3b9e57af4d9395ee24d5" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/386a4b395d45276284606b0f079be16881af717ba7e791ab62d8df34743f0269.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "c631536a9420efbc6a0de73de8e499a41b4407b9198b3b9e57af4d9395ee24d5" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/state.json" "file_sha256" "c631536a9420efbc6a0de73de8e499a41b4407b9198b3b9e57af4d9395ee24d5" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Cou