capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5389.json" "file_sha256" "ad1c0a1002c4997c4c9bc7aeab4540b2b642e73b19319c035786120d2c8a6f8d" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5389.json" "file_sha256" "ad1c0a1002c4997c4c9bc7aeab4540b2b642e73b19319c035786120d2c8a6f8d" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5390.json" "file_sha256" "e853fdf0962cbab977cdd075a7bd1364617e8bad244b02963678d329cc3d58cd" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5390.json" "file_sha256" "e853fdf0962cbab977cdd075a7bd1364617e8bad244b02963678d329cc3d58cd" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5391.json" "file_sha256" "87fae17c40c8ed08fe969a3f66277bd4bf600c1dda8c4eee7ab016d9ef1919d8" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5391.json" "file_sha256" "87fae17c40c8ed08fe969a3f66277bd4bf600c1dda8c4eee7ab016d9ef1919d8" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/728f7aad12a4f127cc002bc38c46fa87ba70d6ecd79ef311a0154014b649a0ce.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5392.json" "file_sha256" "38860f750ac39bb6587f1ecf4a081f338313cae6903df2db525a0555f7bd8be9" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/example-5392.json" "file_sha256" "38860f750ac39bb6587f1ecf4a081f338313cae6903df2db525a0555f7bd8be9" "basis" 867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "30b56f7229fc3a24445181ae29d859922be987891f01ce8c3cf42dccc86d3256" "basis" 1440 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/state.json" "file_sha256" "30b56f7229fc3a24445181ae29d859922be987891f01ce8c3cf42dccc86d3256" "basis" 1440 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate 