erence/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-120653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-120653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-40913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/19f94b8985a4b8da7899f984e0e1c3f88668aa59531658cd0118edb35aab1107.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-40913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{-6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-40913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c83e68e27887c9b817086ec5abfdcf4882c2088e2f49f1961901c05fac5e67b8"} {"question" "q-40913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-40913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-40913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-17161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/11a75207b0adee9fad857aa93f15772e730716b7cc9d5fae7ba39fc5f55418f0.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(5,-5\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-17161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-5\\sqrt{3}}{5}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-17161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficu