ta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-274767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c83e68e27887c9b817086ec5abfdcf4882c2088e2f49f1961901c05fac5e67b8"} {"question" "q-274767" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-274767" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-41263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/34b473c8e212297d8cb434fe0a2dd9872cfa2fda924ac3a4d528bc1318af8c5f.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=8$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-w