pture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c83e68e27887c9b817086ec5abfdcf4882c2088e2f49f1961901c05fac5e67b8"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-12\\sqrt{3},-12)"] ["math" "(-4\\sqrt{3},-4)"] ["math" "(-6\\sqrt{2},-8)"] ["math" "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)"] ["math" "(-8\\sqrt{3},-8)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-120719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/386a4b395d45276284606b0f079be16881af717ba7e791ab62d8df34743f0269.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c83e68e27887c9b817086ec5abfdcf4882c2088e2f49f1961901c05fac5e67b8"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3,-3\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(-3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(-3\\sqrt{3},3)"] ["math" "(3,-3\\sqrt{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-87139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-321972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/6aeb571c312e4dcc0f9ffc4fe2ad9b1a1db5cf02a2f757b66191e31337a8c498.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6\\sqrt{2}$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-321972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-321972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c83e68e27887c9b817086ec5abfdcf4882c2088e2f49f1961901c05fac5e67b8"} {"question" "q-321972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-321972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-321972" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "q-321972" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/state.json" "file_sha256" "95e9b903228e412bab0e16d8a3e3c7698d075032390af8f46cb4dc0ce13f6e11"} {"question" "e-5389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5389.json" "file_sha256" "725b7b55e4898052597ce72a5a2d06570ecbd3e72a60b58c29bec4b5ad94a594"} {"question" "e-5389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5389.json" "file_sha256" "725b7b55e4898052597ce72a5a2d06570ecbd3e72a60b58c29bec4b5ad94a594"} {"question" "e-5390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5390.json" "file_sha256" "4a389627683b1b8bc11bd3fa11993c7f77fd28fa1a3b10b2b2d665d2b01d7b28"} {"question" "e-5390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5390.json" "file_sha256" "4a389627683b1b8bc11bd3fa11993c7f77fd28fa1a3b10b2b2d665d2b01d7b28"} {"question" "e-5391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5391.json" "file_sha256" "1ccb52641004054a9dbefbadc748c23808ee0b97476cac61af4f04b07295791b"} {"question" "e-5391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5391.json" "file_sha256" "1ccb52641004054a9dbefbadc748c23808ee0b97476cac61af4f04b07295791b"} {"question" "e-5392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/728f7aad12a4f127cc002bc38c46fa87ba70d6ecd79ef311a0154014b649a0ce.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5392.json" "file_sha256" "499589ba82a3217d0a453ac3f1161f2d598c023657c1eaf6bc31b4685fa80c49"} {"question" "e-5392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/example-5392.json" "file_sha256" "499589ba82a3217d0a453ac3f1161f2d598c023657c1eaf6bc31b4685fa80c49"}] "usage" {"q-8616" [] "q-40827" [] "q-41263" [] "q-17151" [] "e-5392" [] "e-5389" [] "q-87139" [] "e-5391" [] "q-120719" [] "e-5390" []} "basis" 1453 "retractions" [[17592186134246 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $θ = \\frac{5π}{8}$?"] [17592186280828 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/4a908a59498e1bccef0601ce813ef0a4fa2b7e965c944c9951b13a9f50256459.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?"] [17592186280873 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/e76766c18330d476af9253ecb2d08540df06146a9ac2fdc6bfbe6f8157201f6b.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?"] [17592186134265 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/34b473c8e212297d8cb434fe0a2dd9872cfa2fda924ac3a4d528bc1318af8c5f.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=8$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$"] [17592186307697 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$"] [17592186280887 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/386a4